Source-tracking-breakpoints
----------------------------

- better matching strategy - find suitable fuzzy matching algorithm, changing the lines immediately before the breakpoint, if condition might be slightly wrong

  + I know nothing about this kind of fuzzy-matching, but we ideally
    want something that can handle lines in the context changing, and
    new lines being added within the context window.

    I do wonder if there's any scope to expose the internals of the
    source tracking through the Python API and then allow for
    proto-typing of source tracking algorithms in Python.

- set context and searching window by the user

  + This might have an influence on, and be influenced by, the better
    matching strategy.  If the better strategy requires more context
    then we don't want to allow users to reduce the context below that
    threshold.

    Similarly, though having knobs to controls these things might be
    interesting, we should keep in mind if it is actually _useful_ to
    the end users.  Ideally we'd find a context size that's "good
    enough" and just stick with it.  The ideal solution (IMHO) is not
    having a user setting -- though this doesn't prevent us having a
    maintenance setting.

- flag to create a particular breakpoint without tracking

  + I'm thinking something like "break -source-tracking on|off ...."
    though we need to think about what to do if a user specifically
    asks for source tracking and then tries to create a breakpoint
    that cannot be source tracked, e.g. "break -source-tracking on
    function_name" should this give an error?  Just ignore the flag?

- Breakpoint checking. create bp, symtab + line list, can we track? when we reload, we take the location spec and recreate the bp locations, we create symtab and linelist again, has bfd changes? then we need to read the source code and find a new location and then create a new symtab and line location, what if the location spec does not match any source line, then we won't get any symtab and line, if that happens, the breakpoint will become disabled as the line won't match anything, we want a solution when we're not relying on using the original the orig location spec in order to figure out if the bp needs to be updated. When we originally capture the src context, we should stat() the source file, we know it was last changed, when we recreate the bp, we can stat again and we know if it got changed. We need to store the objfile and bfd.

  + Consider an input file:

    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    static int
    some_function (void)
    {
      return 0;		/* Line 4.  */
    }

    int
    main (void)
    {
      return some_function ();
    }
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    In this example, place a source tracking breakpoint on line 4.
    Now edit the file to look like this:

    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    /* Comment.
       Comment.
       Comment.  */
    static int
    some_function (void)
    {
      return 0;		/* Line 4.  */
    }

    int
    main (void)
    {
      return some_function ();
    }
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    When you restart GDB the source-tracking breakpoint will fail to
    update, and you'll get an error like:

    Error in re-setting breakpoint 1: No compiled code for line 4 in file "source-tracking.c".

    This is because, in order to update a source tracking breakpoint
    GDB first creates the symtab_and_line objects for the old
    location, and from that tries to figure out if the location needs
    to move.  In the above case there is no compiled code for the old
    location after the change.  If instead you change the source file
    to:

    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    static int
    some_function (void)
    {
      /* Comment.
         Comment.
         Comment.  */
      return 0;		/* Line 4.  */
    }

    int
    main (void)
    {
      return some_function ();
    }
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Now the breakpoint will update correctly as an attempt to break on
    line 4 is still inside the function.

    Fixing this will require some restructuring within GDB.  Currently
    the source cache is symtab based, e.g. symtab in, source code out,
    but we need it to be filename based, filename in, source code out.

    With that done, the bp_source object needs to track more state,
    including the actual source filename, along with (I think) all the
    BFD objects in which breakpoints have been placed.

    We can then ask "has the source file changed?"  If not then
    nothing to do.  If the source file has changed then "has the BFD
    also changed?"  If it has then we might need to update the
    breakpoint location.  Grab the source code from the new cache
    using just the filename and pass it to the location tracking code.

    Get back a new breakpoint line number and update the breakpoint
    location spec (as we currently do).

    Only now should we try to create the breakpoint symtab_and_line
    objects from the breakpoint spec.

- turning on by default blocker - tracking line might get changed as the algorithm gets better, we would need to update the testsuite - do we need to print anything at all? maybe we need a helper function to match from info breakpoints?

  + The current text is "source-tracking enabled (tracking 3 lines
    around line 8)" or similar.  My take is that everything in the
    parenthesis is actually an implementation detail that the user
    shouldn't need to worry about.  If this feature "just works" then
    the user shouldn't need to worry about this detail.

    The "around line 8" part is already duplication of the breakpoint
    location, i.e. the line above this says: "... in <function> at
    <file>:8" so that's one part we could definitely drop.

    And I don't see why the user should care about the context size.
    If this feature requires the user to micro-manage the context size
    then this feature isn't working as well as I'd like.  The user
    just wants this to work.

    I do think that we should indicate when a breakpoint is source
    tracked though, so I'd just reduce this to "source-tracking
    enabled".

